==English==
{{wp}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Napoleon}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|Napoléon||given name, usually in reference to Napoleon Bonaparte}}. In reference to coins, via {{bor|en|fr|napoléon||(slang) a coin that originally had the appearance of Napoleon's face in the obverse}}.<ref>"[http://www.oed.com/view/Entry/258537 Napoleon, ''n.¹'']" in the ''Oxford English Dictionary'' (2003), Oxford: Oxford University Press.</ref> In reference to guns and possibly cards, in reference to {{w|Napoleon III}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/nəˈpəʊlɪən/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/nəˈpoʊli.ən/|/nəˈpoʊljn/|/nəˈpoʊljən/|a=US}}
*: {{rhymes|en|əʊliən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical|_|numismatics|slang|nocat=y}} The [[franc germinal]]: a 20-[[franc]] [[gold coin]] [[issue]]d under {{w|Napoleon I of France}}.
# {{lb|en|historical|_|numismatics|slang|nocat=y}} Other [[subsequent]] 20-[[franc]] [[coin]]s, [[note]]s, or [[value]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=w:Arthur Conan Doyle|title=w:The Adventure of the Red-Headed League|publisher=Norton|year_published=2005|page=67
|passage=We had occasion some months ago to strengthen our resources, and borrowed, for that purpose, thirty thousand '''napoleons''' from the Bank of France.}}
# {{lb|en|usually|_|admiringly|_|pejorative}} A person [[resembling]] {{w|Napoleon Bonaparte}}, (usually) in having [[come]] to [[dominate]] an [[area]] or [[sphere]] of [[activity]] through [[ruthlessness]] or [[illegality]] [[as well as]] [[surpassing]] [[ability]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1894|author=w:Arthur Conan Doyle|title=The Memoirs of Sherlock Holmes|page=260
|passage=He [''[[sc.]]'' {{w|James Moriarty}}] is the '''Napoleon''' of crime, [[w:John Watson|Watson]]. He is the organizer of half that is evil and of nearly all that is undetected in this great city [''sc.'' [[London]]]. He is a genius, a philosopher, an abstract thinker.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1896|author=J.S. Ogilvie|title=The Life and Speeches of William McKinley
|passage=He [''[[sc.]]'' {{w|William McKinley|McKinley}}] has been called the '''Napoleon''' of protection, as other men have been called '''Napoleons''' of finance.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=w:Ben Macintyre|title=The '''Napoleon''' of Crime: The Life and Times of {{w|Adam Worth}}, Master Thief}}
# {{lb|en|fashion|obsolete}} {{clipping of|en|Napoleon boot|nodot=a}}: a [[form]] of [[topboot]] [[worn]] by [[officer]]s during the {{w|Napoleonic Wars}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Robert Morgan|title=Lions of the West:|passage=Always an eloquent speaker, [[w:James K. Polk|Polk]] became known as the '''Napoleon''' of the stump.}}
# {{lb|en|historical|_|weaponry}} {{clipping of|en|Napoleon gun|nodot=a}}: a 12-[[pound]]er [[cannon]] [[first]] [[use]]d by the Second [[French]] Empire.
# {{lb|en|card games|uncountable}} A 19th-[[century]] five-card [[trick]]-[[taking]] [[card game|game]] [[simplified]] from [[euchre]].
# {{lb|en|card games|uncountable}} {{clipping of|en|double napoleon|nodot=a}}: a [[form]] of [[patience]].
# {{lb|en|rare|_|UK|_|horse racing|_|slang|obsolete}} {{alt form|en|nap|nodot=a}}: a [[horse]] to [[go nap on]], a [[sure thing]], a [[certain]] [[winner]]; a [[prediction]] of [[such]] a horse; a [[bet]] on [[such]] a horse.
# {{clipping of|en|Napoleon bigarreau|nodot=a}}: a [[variety]] of the [[white]]-fleshed [[bigarreau]] [[cherry]]; a [[variety]] of [[cherry tree]] [[bear]]ing [[such]] [[fruit]].
# {{lb|en|food|originally|_|US|_|slang}} {{clipping of|en|Napoleon cake|nodot=a}}: [[another]] [[name]] for a [[millefeuille]] [[pastry]].
#* {{RQ:Vance Nobody|chapter=Anarchy|page=17|passage=Fascinated, Miss Manvers thrust aside a garnished plate, took a gulp of the decoction called coffee, and attacked her '''napoleon'''.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} {{clipping of|en|Napoleon brandy|nodot=a}}: any excellent [[brandy]]; a [[serving]] of [[such]] a [[brandy]].
# {{short for|en|Napoleon slice}}

